epartment because of fever, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and\ndecreased urine output over the past 48 hours. She has an 8-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus treated\nwith glipizide. She has no history of renal disease. Her temperature is 39°C (102.2°F), and respirations are\n24/min. While supine, her pulse is 106/min, and blood pressure is 116/70 mm Hg. While sitting, her pulse is\n120/min, and blood pressure is 88/58 mm Hg. She appears acutely ill and slightly lethargic. Examination\nshows decreased skin turgor, dry mucous membranes, and flat neck veins. There is mild, generalized\nabdominal tenderness without guarding or rebound tenderness. Laboratory studies show:\nLeukocyte count\n15,000/mm5\nSerum\nNa*\n146 mEq/L\nGi=\n100 mEq/L\nKr\n2.9 mEq/L\nHCO,-\n28 mEq/L\nUrea nitrogen\n72 mg/dL\nGlucose\n254 mg/d_L\nCreatinine\n3.4 mg/dL\nUrine\nNa*\n8 mEq/L\nPH\n5.0\nSpecific gravity\n1.028\nGlucose\n2+\nKetones\ntrace\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Acute interstitial nephritis"},{"id":"B","text":"Acute tubular necrosis"},{"id":"C","text":"Obstructive uropathy"},{"id":"D","text":"Postinfectious glomerulonephritis"},{"id":"E","text":"Prerenal azotemia"}],"explanation":"","correctOptionId":null,"visualsReviewed":false,"duplicatesReviewed":false},"extraction_confidence":"0.45","answer_status":"UNVERIFIED","review_status":"NEEDS_REVIEW","review_notes":"Recovered from previously unrepresented OCR PDF page. Unverified automated extraction: manually compare question, options, images, and answer key with original source."},{"source_id":"fad13796-e948-49ef-8a20-c3c736ad53d5","page_start":171,"page_end":171,"original_question_no":"49","content_fingerprint":"9c0227d1f940322c12a8bb54ce4cad3b","draft_payload":{"text":"49.\nA47-year-old man comes to the physician because of a 1-month history of progressive swelling of both legs\nand generalized weakness. He says he now has some difficulty performing his usual activities. Last year, he\nreceived the diagnosis of hepatitis B and C. He has a long-standing history of intravenous drug use. He\nrefused treatment for hepatitis C and currently takes no medications. He is not in acute distress. He is\n183 cm (6 ft) tall and weighs 55 kg (121\nIb); BMI is 16 kg/m2. His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is\n7O/min, respirations are 12/min, and blood pressure is 160/99 mm Hg. Cardiopulmonary examination shows\nno abnormalities. The abdomen is nontender. There is no hepatomegaly. Muscle strength is normal.\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin\n10 g/dL\nLeukocyte count\n93000/mm=?\nPlatelet count\n260,000/mm>?\nSerum\nUrea nitrogen\n22 mg/dL\nCreatinine\n2.1 mg/dL\nAlbumin\n3.8 g/dL\nTotal bilirubin\n0.6 mg/dL\nAST\n7O U/L\nALT\n60 U/L\nUrine\nBlood\n3+\nProtein\n4+\nMicroscopic examination of urine shows numerous RBCs, which are mostly acanthocytes with irregular\nmembrane protrusions. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?","options":[{"id":"A","text":"Second set of laboratory studies in\n1 month"},{"id":"B","text":"Urine protein electrophoresis"},{"id":"C","text":"Urine culture"},{"id":"D","text":"Cystoscopy"},{"id":"E","text":"Renal biopsy"}],"explanation":"","correctOptionId":null,"visualsReviewed":false,"duplicatesReviewed":false},"extraction_confidence":"0.45","answer_status":"UNVERIFIED","review_status":"NEEDS_REVIEW","review_notes":"Recovered from previously unrepresented OCR PDF page. Unverified automated extraction: manually compare question, options, images, and answer key with original source."}]'::jsonb) AS t(
